{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Cojocaru2004-QWDW-N48-npa04",
  "Source": "E. Cojocaru, \"Quarter-wave discrete wavelet design of dichroic highly reflecting-transmitting mirrors for ultrafast solid-state lasers,\" Appl. Opt. 43, 1261-1265 (2004). doi:10.1364/AO.43.001261 - Sec. 4, p. 1263 (text below Fig. 4) with Eqs. (2) and (3): N = 48, na = 1.9, npa = 0.4, n0 = ns = na; lambda0 = 1 um assumed",
  "Notes": "QWDW example quoted in Sec. 4: na = 1.9, npa = 0.4, N = 48, said to reach R0 >= 0.9999\nSpecification: Smallest QWDW with R0 >= 0.9999 at na = 1.9, npa = 0.4.\nReported: Sec. 4: 'at na = 1.9 and npa = 0.4, with a QWDW, one achieves R0 >= 0.9999 at N >= 48' (right-hand curve 2 of Fig. 4). Eq. (7) with f = 0.5164 gives N ~ 48.5. Stop-band width at R = 0.5 from Eq. (4): dl/l0 ~ (4/pi)(0.4/1.9) = 0.268. CAUTION: with the assumed mid-layer sampling this stack gives R0 = 0.999853 at lambda0, not >= 0.9999 (that sampling first reaches 0.9999 at N = 50, R0 = 0.999904); other simple samplings (layer edges, T = (N +/- 1) lambda0/4) give 0.99982-0.99988 at N = 48, so the paper's exact discretisation is not recoverable from the text.\nNotes: Same construction and assumptions as Cojocaru2004-QWDW-N50-npa035 (n_i = na + (-1)^(i+1) npa A(x_i), x_i = (i - 1/2) lambda0/4, T = 12 um, i counted from the substrate, list reversed to incident-first). lambda0 is not stated for this example; R0 at lambda0 does not depend on it because all layers are QW. Normal incidence; incident medium and substrate both n = na = 1.9 (n0 = ns = na), non-dispersive and lossless, so R is the same whichever side faces the light.\nLayers from Sec. 4, p. 1263 (text below Fig. 4) with Eqs. (2) and (3): N = 48, na = 1.9, npa = 0.4, n0 = ns = na; lambda0 = 1 um assumed; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), differs from the paper: ours R0 0.99985257 at 1000 nm; paper R0 >= 0.9999 from N = 48 (Sec. 4). The paper does not say where it samples the wavelet A(x); at the middle of each layer, as here, R0 reaches 0.9999 only at N = 50 (layer edges and a stretched profile do no better). The paper’s own Eq. 7 gives N ≈ 48.5, so its N ≥ 48 is probably rounded.",
  "Wavelength": [
    1000
  ],
